WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:06.340
We are now ready to solve the Peptide
Identification Problem, which is:

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:11.860
Find a peptide from a proteome with
a maximum score against a spectrum.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.170
The input to the problem
is a spectral vector and

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:20.850
an amino acid string Proteome, and the
output is an amino acid string Peptide

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.980
that maximizes the score
between peptide and

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:27.860
spectral vector on all
substrings of Proteome.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.270
However, even if we solve this problem,

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:37.640
how will it help us, since we don't
know what the T-Rex Proteome is?

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.446
However, we can approximate
T-Rex Proteome.

00:00:41.446 --> 00:00:48.270
Note that 90% of proteins making
up animal bones are collagens,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:54.480
and collagens are very conserved across
species, and therefore, collagens in T-Rex

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.280
and will likely be similar to
collagens in some present day species,

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:01.978
the question is to which ones.

00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:07.850
And therefore, Asara formed
a database of collagen proteins,

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:13.040
but as a sanity check, he actually
compared the T-Rex spectra against

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.950
the entire UniProt database of
all proteins known, which

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:23.830
currently consists of 200 million
amino acids from hundreds of species.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:28.950
Asara also included some mutated version
of collagens from present day species.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:35.000
We will call the augmented
database UniProt+.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.010
Afterwards, Asara searched all T-Rex
spectra against the UniProt+ database, and

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:47.790
it turns out that most of the high scoring
peptides identified in UniProt+

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:52.820
were chicken collagens, or
were very similar to chicken collagens,

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:58.190
supporting the hypothesis that
birds evolved from dinosaurs.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:04.500
For example, this is one of the peptides
identified by Asara for a dinosaur spectrum,

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.250
and this peptide is
only one mutation away,

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.760
shown in red,
from the chicken collagen peptide.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.790
But how can we be sure
that this DinosaurPeptide

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:19.380
is the correct interpretation
of DinosaurSpectrum?

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.955
Shouldn't we analyze the statistical
significance of DinosaurPeptide?

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:29.575
In fact, DinosaurPeptide, indeed,
is the highest scoring peptide for

00:02:29.575 --> 00:02:35.420
DinosaurSpectrum among all
peptides in UnitProt+.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:39.640
But the surprising fact is that
there are billions of peptides

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.410
not occurring in UnitProt+
that outscore DinosaurPeptide.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.370
Does this concern you?

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.740
It looks like we need
to develop a method for

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:59.030
evaluating the statistical
significance of identified peptides.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:03.865
And to help us with evaluating the statistical
significance of DinosaurPeptide,

00:03:03.865 --> 00:03:06.460
we need to recall the small print.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:13.180
The match between Peptide and
Spectrum was considered significant if

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:18.240
the resulting score is sufficiently high,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.890
and therefore, we defined the notion
of Peptide Spectrum Match as follows:

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:29.425
Given a parameter "threshold,"
a peptide, and a spectral vector,

00:03:29.425 --> 00:03:36.560
form a Peptide-Spectrum Match,
abbreviated as PSM, if Peptide

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.570
is a highest-scoring peptide against
Spectrum among all peptides in Proteome.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:43.428
But that's not all.

00:03:43.428 --> 00:03:48.390
In addition, we will require
the score between Peptide and

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.200
Spectrum to be larger or
equal to the threshold.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:58.780
And after we define the notion of PSM,
we will define the set

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:03.960
of PSMs derived from Proteome and
SpectralVector and

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:09.490
a given threshold as the set of all
Peptide-Spectrum matches resulting

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:14.730
from a set of SpectralVectors for
a given Proteome and a given threshold.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:22.270
Our PSM search problem now is to
identify all Peptide-Spectrum Matches

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:27.450
scoring above a threshold for
a set of spectra and a proteome.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:32.200
Input is a set SpectralVectors,
an amino acid string Proteome, and

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.300
a score threshold.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:38.570
And the Output is the set of
Peptide-Spectrum Matches define for

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:42.766
Proteome, SpectralVectors,
and the parameter threshold.

